<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brihadishvara Temple</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1010</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Rajarajeswaram, Peruvudaiyar Kovil</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Brihadishvara Temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaraja-i">Rajaraja I</a> and completed in 1010. Its granite tower, about 66 metres high, was among the tallest buildings of its time, and its walls carry Chola inscriptions and frescoes. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, part of the Great Living Chola Temples.</p></div>
